<figure class="case-lead-image case-lead-burgundy"><img src="/img/DAAG/institutional-consulting-presentation-title-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" alt="Designing for equitable digital access presentation title."></figure>

<section class="case-summary"><div><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>The audience needed to understand access risks well enough to decide when and where to act.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>This self-initiated example explores communication for technical advisory work. A list of access risks can create awareness, but it does not show relative priority or where intervention belongs in a programme.</p><p>The presentation therefore moves through three jobs: establish why design-stage decisions matter, prioritise specific risks and locate advisory input across the lifecycle.</p></div></section>

<section class="decision-band"><span>03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">The decision</p><h2>Turn a broad concern into a sequence of practical choices.</h2></div></section>

<section class="case-gallery">
  <figure class="gallery-wide"><img src="/img/DAAG/structured-list-consulting-presentation-slide-layout.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Two-column structure connecting design conditions and access consequences."><figcaption><strong>Reframe the issue.</strong> Parallel lists connect early design assumptions with later access consequences.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/DAAG/risk-assessment-framework-consulting-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Access risk definition and examples."><figcaption><strong>Define the risk.</strong> The framework establishes a shared language before asking for prioritisation.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/DAAG/risk-matrix-2x2-quadrant-consulting-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Severity and likelihood matrix for access risks."><figcaption><strong>Prioritise action.</strong> Severity and likelihood separate urgent risks from those to monitor.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/DAAG/project-lifecycle-timeline-infographic-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Programme lifecycle diagram locating advisory input."><figcaption><strong>Show where to intervene.</strong> The lifecycle makes the value of early engagement explicit.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/DAAG/before-after-case-study-consulting-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Before-and-after comparison of programme assumptions."><figcaption><strong>Make change tangible.</strong> A before-and-after view translates a principle into concrete programme decisions.</figcaption></figure>
</section>

<section class="case-outcome"><div><p class="eyebrow">The practical output</p><h2>An editable presentation and a set of tools for review and discussion.</h2></div><p>This is self-initiated work, created to demonstrate how our <a href="/services/information-design/">information design</a> can communicate technical advisory ideas. The risk matrix can support a workshop; the lifecycle diagram can help commissioners decide when to engage expertise.</p></section>

